Investigation evidence chain — SideCamera AC-4 timing — revision 5

Scope: the accepted single-camera AC-4 maximum-gap failure. The accepted five-minute reproduction remains unchanged and was not rerun. Observations are listed before inference.

1. Observed — accepted five-minute current-source capture (2026-11-15 08:15:26–08:20:31 UTC). It recorded 6,050 matched frames over 302.506666 seconds; 6,049 EOF intervals averaged 49.998692 ms (20.000523 fps), maximum 89.163715 ms, and 753 intervals exceeded 60 ms. This remains an AC-4 max-gap failure. `evidence/reproduction.log` is byte-identical to `../build/evidence/runtime-smoke-ac4-r2.2-round2.log`, SHA-256 `de804d35326c6925862d2c8b36708d4a14a10b05120076924f523397976e8c55`. Product source `system/sidecamerad.py` remains SHA-256 `494fb8b88fda8bcf1d8301225019f4f01f4b77a0bb5cb3e69dde47ed96e1309b`.

2. Observed — corrected same-run DQBUF/PTS/output-write/read correlation (2026-11-15 10:00:10 UTC). After startup: DQBUF intervals mean 49.994824 ms, max 58.634 ms, zero >60 ms; SOE PTS intervals mean 50.006737 ms, max 52.128 ms, zero >60 ms; output write-boundary intervals mean 49.957065 ms, max 89.580 ms, 146/598 >60 ms; Python full-read intervals mean 49.957056 ms, max 89.587 ms, 147/598 >60 ms. Write-start to read completion was median 0.116 ms and p95 0.196 ms. There were 602 successful DQBUF calls for 600 output frames, and sequence fields were not captured; exact dequeue-to-output identity is not proven. Source report `../build/reports/report_dqbuf-output-correlation-r2.3-r1.md` SHA-256 `6d96162d197305e68eb88c8ae29cd8f67f957930c627ce01d4d8932318336c5a`; artifact `../build/artifacts/dqbuf-output-correlation-r2.3-r1.json` SHA-256 `29c587a2bf121c8e35c16481400f621686505cf3ad3afe0f95cb7f0ffab130b2`.

3. Observed — corrected output write syscall durations: 25,800 writes, median 0.058 ms, p95 0.153 ms, p99 0.256 ms, maximum 10.554 ms, none >50 ms. This does not support sustained pipe backpressure. The separate writes-only trace was observer-intrusive and unpaired with DQBUF; it remains corroboration only.

4. Observed — per-thread schedstat probe (2026-11-15 10:20:43 UTC): 600/600 frames; 77/599 read intervals >60 ms; maximum 80.383947 ms. Four FFmpeg threads exposed schedstat. Long-gap intervals averaged 1.469 ms summed runqueue delay versus 1.273 ms on the other intervals; only 3/77 long gaps had any thread delay >=5 ms. This does not support CPU runqueue delay as the primary cause. Post-read `/proc` snapshots add observer cost to the next interval and do not identify the exact output writer thread. Artifact `artifacts/ffmpeg-schedstat-600-r2.json` SHA-256 `e3bea2a7874fdb5e6b98d6aeb7fbaeca65308845feb684883d8491e11b01aa36`.

5. Observed — output-flush ABBA diagnostic (A1 default 2026-11-15 11:23:18 UTC; B1 flush 11:23:49 UTC; B2 flush 11:24:20 UTC; A2 default 11:24:51 UTC). Each sequential traced arm read 600/600 frames and emitted 25,800 writes; no frame payload was saved and source hashes matched. Pooled read gaps >60 ms: A 228/1,198, maximum 90.374 ms; B 227/1,198, maximum 96.677 ms. Pooled write-boundary start gaps >60 ms: A 228/1,198, maximum 90.280 ms; B 228/1,198, maximum 96.578 ms. B1 alone looked better than A1, but B2 was worse than A2; pooled counts are effectively unchanged and B maxima are higher. This does not reproduce a repeatable cadence improvement from `-flush_packets 1`. All arms were sequential and straced; ABBA bounds simple drift but order/run and observer effects remain. Report `../build/reports/report_ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.md` SHA-256 `e904cc3db831e98eb200e7bdd988b333082ccacfa4df4a85daa2410d94259ca2`; JSON `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.json` SHA-256 `db777e8aa2d4b63155e5595cda47cfa863367805885c270f518df3dde7389671`; summary log `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.log` SHA-256 `7816977983473254c5fc01747f84a1d33d07b35869b3021a425128363ace0064`. Raw trace hashes: A1 `e9609a2446d0fe009059919d8f6a363e3f73d3263b35d65b9993b9b9225be0e3`, B1 `4dac35c98aed5615a9ad45e32748ffd79835ce1231b3191dc3bfc215353c132a`, B2 `8e686f905fbf4021ab9b717d469d17d48794922a3c5c74e27e8f0649a0ae0d23`, A2 `6d6e937c8fc8c19a889d9311be01416cf0531d3625cf0abe1eabeba1fe784044`.

Inference (medium confidence): timing divergence remains after V4L2 dequeue and before raw-pipe delivery, inside FFmpeg processing/output. Reader-side backlog, CPU runqueue delay as the primary cause, and sustained pipe backpressure are not supported. The ABBA result makes the earlier single-pair `-flush_packets 1` improvement non-repeatable; this setting is not a supported fix. Exact internal decode/filter/output pacing or synchronization wait remains unknown. PTS are V4L2 SOE, not EOF; keep `timestampEof` as host-monotonic time sampled after the complete raw-frame read.


6. Observed — offline futex wait/wake pairing against the existing final2 syscall trace (no capture rerun). Parsing found 8,544 futex calls with all unfinished/resumed pairs closed, zero pending or unparsed. Output-writer TID 863811's 598 successful FUTEX_WAIT calls lasting at least 1 ms (592 at least 10 ms) each had a positive same-address FUTEX_WAKE syscall from FFmpeg leader TID 863769 temporally inside the wait interval; 299 temporal pairs occurred at each of two alternating addresses. This is not kernel-confirmed waiter identity: FUTEX_WAKE does not report which waiter it woke, and a concurrent same-address waiter may have been selected. Median wake-to-wait-completion time was 0.201 ms. Correlation analysis separately reports that all 133 read gaps >60 ms overlapped >=1 ms futex waits by each of TIDs 863769, 863808, and 863811, and all 133 gaps overlapped at least one futex wait >=10 ms. TID 863811 made all 25,800 fd 12 output writes; DQBUF calls were issued by 863769 and 863809. This is syscall/thread correlation; it does not establish what the futex condition represents or prove that the wake caused the delayed output. The output writer may be idle awaiting upstream work. Offline pairing report `evidence/ffmpeg-futex-wake-pairing-r5.log` and structured result `artifacts/ffmpeg-futex-wake-pairing-r5.json` bind these counts and limitations. The strace and post-read schedstat observer overhead remains uncalibrated, and no stack/wchan was captured.

Inference (medium confidence): jitter remains after V4L2 dequeue and within FFmpeg processing/output before raw-pipe delivery. Futex pairing strengthens localization to FFmpeg internal synchronization as a correlated wait, without resolving whether it is upstream readiness, decode/filter synchronization, or another condition. Reader backlog, primary CPU runqueue delay, and sustained pipe backpressure remain unsupported. PTS are V4L2 SOE, not EOF; `timestampEof` remains host-monotonic time at full raw-frame read completion.
